Combine what you now know about the alert job. Given the original forEach version:

```ts
async function runAlertJob() {
  const searches = await db.savedSearch.findMany({ where: { alertsEnabled: true } });
  searches.forEach(async (search) => {
    const matches = await findNewMatches(search);
    if (matches.length > 0) {
      await sendAlertEmail(search.userId, matches);
    }
  });
  await db.alertRun.create({ data: { completedAt: new Date() } });
}
```

write a corrected version that fixes the unawaited-forEach race AND the N+1 query in findNewMatches AND replaces the swallow-and-continue error handling with something that records failures without crashing the whole run. You don't need to implement retry/backoff in full — a comment describing where it would go is enough. Explain, for each of the three fixes, what production symptom it prevents.

explanation:
A corrected version:
```ts
async function runAlertJob() {
  const jobStartTime = new Date();
  const searches = await db.savedSearch.findMany({ where: { alertsEnabled: true } });

  // Fetch new jobs once — fixes the N+1: one query instead of one per saved search.
  const newJobs = await db.job.findMany({ where: { postedAt: { gt: globalWatermark } } });

  for (const search of searches) {
    // for...of + await fixes the race: each search's send fully completes
    // before the loop moves on, so alertRun.create() below can't run early.
    const matches = newJobs.filter(j => j.title.includes(search.keyword) && j.postedAt > search.lastAlertAt);
    if (matches.length === 0) continue;
    try {
      await sendAlertEmail(search.userId, matches);
      // NOTE: retry with exponential backoff + jitter would go here,
      // capped at a few attempts, before falling through to the catch.
    } catch (err) {
      await db.failedSend.create({ data: { searchId: search.id, error: String(err), occurredAt: new Date() } });
    }
  }

  await db.alertRun.create({ data: { completedAt: new Date() } });
}
```
What each fix prevents: replacing `forEach` with an awaited `for...of` prevents `alertRun.create()` from running before sends have actually finished, which is what caused duplicate alert emails when the job was re-triggered mid-run. Moving the 'new jobs' query outside the loop prevents the job's runtime and database load from scaling with the number of saved searches (50,000 queries) instead of the number of new postings, which is what made the job slow — and eventually timeout-prone — as the saved-search count grew. Recording each failure in a `failedSend` table instead of only logging it prevents a user's missed alert from vanishing with no trace, and — because the catch is now inside the per-search loop rather than wrapping the whole run — one user's failed send no longer stops every subsequent user in the batch from being processed.

